---
source: crates/skif-cli/tests/cli.rs
expression: help.stdout
---
Command-line interface for skif, a dependency and package-manager linter for JS/TS monorepos.

Usage: skif [OPTIONS] [PATH]
       skif <COMMAND>

Commands:
  rules          List every rule with its category, default level and fixability
  schema         Print the JSON Schema of `skif.toml`
  init           Write a starter `skif.toml`; refuses to overwrite an existing configuration
  config         Show the configuration files a root is built from, the merged result, and where each key was set
  explain-usage  Explain why the JavaScript analysis counts a file, or one export of it, as used
  migrate        Translate the configuration of sherif, syncpack and knip into a `skif.toml`; never deletes a file
  list           List every dependency the version groups govern, by version group, with its specifiers and states
  json           Print every dependency instance the version groups govern as one JSON object per line (NDJSON)
  help           Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Arguments:
  [PATH]
          A path inside a project; the workspace root is the nearest ancestor workspace that lists the project as a member, else the project itself. Without a `package.json` here or above, every project below is linted
          
          [default: .]

Options:
  -r, --recursive
          Lint every workspace and standalone package at or below PATH, each with the `skif.toml` files that govern it

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format [default: human, or human plus GitHub annotations when `GITHUB_ACTIONS=true`]

          Possible values:
          - human:  Grouped, optionally coloured text
          - json:   One JSON document on stdout
          - sarif:  SARIF 2.1.0 for code scanning
          - github: GitHub Actions workflow-command annotations

      --output <FILE>
          Write the rendering to this file instead of stdout

      --fix
          Apply safe fixes, then lint again and run the package manager's install command

      --fix-unsafe
          Apply safe and unsafe fixes; implies --fix

      --dry-run
          With --fix or --fix-unsafe: print the planned changes as a unified diff (JSON: the `fix` object with `changes`) and write nothing; no install runs

      --select <SELECT>
          Which version a version alignment fix targets, overriding `[policy] prefer` for this run
          
          [possible values: highest, lowest]

      --no-install
          Do not run the package manager after fixing

      --max-warnings <N>
          Exit 1 when there are more than N warnings (counted over all roots)

      --fail-on-warnings
          Same as --max-warnings 0

      --no-prompt
          Never ask which alternative fix to apply; the diagnostic stays unfixed unless --select decides

      --rule <ID=LEVEL>
          Set a rule's level, as ID=off|info|warn|error. Repeatable

      --ignore-rule <ID>
          Turn a rule off. Repeatable

      --ignore-package <NAME>
          Ignore a package by name or glob in every rule; replaces the file's list. Repeatable

      --ignore-dependency <NAME>
          Ignore a dependency in every rule. Repeatable

      --ignore-rule-for-package <RULE:PACKAGE>
          Ignore one rule for one package, as RULE:PACKAGE. Repeatable

      --require-ignore-reasons
          Fail when an `[[ignore]]` entry of a configuration file has no `reason`

      --no-js
          Skip JavaScript analysis

      --config <FILE>
          Use this configuration file (and the files it extends) instead of discovering `skif.toml` files

      --no-config
          Read no configuration file: the defaults plus the flags

      --production
          Only analyse production code in JavaScript analysis

      --color <COLOR>
          When to colour human output
          
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, always, never]

      --dependency <NAME>
          Only the dependencies whose name matches this name or glob (`react`, `@types/**`, `!eslint`), in the version-group policies. Repeatable. syncpack's `--dependencies`

      --dependency-types <TYPES>
          Only these dependency types (`prod,dev`, `peer`, `!local`, a custom type), in the version-group policies; also their default scope for this run. Comma separated

      --specifier-types <TYPES>
          Only instances with these specifier types (`exact,range`, `!workspace-protocol`), in the version-group policies. Comma separated

      --source <GLOB>
          Only instances declared in files matching this glob, relative to the root (`packages/app/package.json`), in the version-group policies. Repeatable

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version
